A1. A quiet all-year-round resort. ~ TOMB
A5. This is the way to keep your wife. ~ POOR
A9. Corn fields. ~ FEET
A11. How a strong drink makes you feel. ~ WEAK
A13. When you say this the doctor looks down in the mouth. ~ AH
A15. An afternoon cocktail party. ~ TEA
A16. Workmen do this when the noon whistle blows. ~ EAT
A17. An exclamation. ~ BO
A18. This is going on the rocks. ~ SEA
A20. What politicians are taken for in Chicago. ~ RIDES
A22. The chap who says: "Believe it or not." ~ RIP
A23. The love call of the bathing girl. ~ HELP
A25. Part of a cigarette holder. ~ LIP
A26. What an elephant "ain't." ~ CUTE
A27. This is all shot in Chicago. ~ LEAD
A29. Something awfully nice about your girl. (plural) ~ ARMS
A30. How can you keep this when you are always giving it? ~ PROMISE
A31. A becoming color for a temple. ~ GREY
A33. What politicians do when they run for Congress. ~ PANT
A36. A good thing to show an insurance agent. ~ DOOR
A37. A salad field. ~ ANT
A39. In greater numbers. ~ MORE
A41. A hair mattress. ~ RAT
A42. What the waiter expects at a night club. ~ ORDER
A44. Joined up the aisle. ~ WED
A45. A preposition. ~ AT
A46. A sticky stick. ~ GUM
A47. The flapper's easy chair. ~ LAP
A49. The butcher who uses scissors. (abbr.) ~ ED
A50. How the checkered suit makes the horseman look. ~ RACY
A51. The lad who made a hole in one. ~ LIAR
A53. The lost and found department of a restaurant. ~ HASH
A54. What Clare Briggs did for the Herald-Tribune last week. ~ DREW

D2. This comes after the Prince. ~ OF
D3. What you and the wife did for lunch. ~ MET
D4. The "not quite" beverage. ~ BEER
D5. Restless vegetables. ~ PEAS
D6. Early motor fuel. ~ OAT
D7. All right. (abbr.) ~ OK
D8. What the boys put up on their Florida lots. ~ CASH
D10. This often gets a can on. ~ TAIL
D11. Heroines do this in a movie shed. ~ WEEP
D12. This held the ship in quarantine. ~ ROPE
D14. This makes her a high-stepper. ~ HEEL
D17. Horses' steering gears. ~ BITS
D19. This wears a white collar. ~ ALE
D21. You need this to play solitaire. ~ DIAMOND
D22. Jamaica ginger. ~ RUM
D24. What most tabloid articles are written on. ~ PAPER
D26. If the bottle hasn't this on top it's not genuine. ~ CREAM
D28. Mrs. Jones won't hold your baby unless he's like this. ~ DRY
D29. An Egyptian sheik. ~ ASP
D31. He devours the morning paper. ~ GOAT
D32. An apple a day will do this eventually. ~ ROT
D34. Right off the bat. ~ NOW
D35. What Babe Ruth's bat came from. ~ TREE
D36. Pull. ~ DRAG
D37. This moves on its stomach. ~ ARMY
D38. One thing daisies won't do. ~ TELL
D40. A whirlpool. ~ EDDY
D42. A dental accessory. ~ OUCH
D43. A prohibition gesture. ~ RAID
D46. This will make you laugh. ~ GAS
D48. If your swing is good you can break this. ~ PAR
D50. A god. ~ RA
D52. The second word in a business letter. ~ RE


